A||N||1||Capt Fiero||FW 3.4 conversion info||||1||
Z||000000||Capt Fiero||04-01-2000||02:00 PM||||This post is from Dennis, from another list, but it is a extreme use to anyone that wants to get rid of there stock 2.8 V-6 and upgrade.  I have done a ton of research on this swap as I am going to be doing this swap in April.  <P>Of all of the engine upgrades available the 3.4L  60* V6 pushrod<BR>engine is the easiest to swap.. This is because it is basically a<BR>direct bolt job which uses all of the original Fiero sensors,<BR>hardware, Fuel rail, Plenum, Front Cover,  ECM, wire harness,<BR>bracketry and all the accessories etc.<BR>For list members considering swapping engines for the larger<BR>displacement  3.4L 60* V6 , many of you know that  relocating the<BR>starter to the opposite side of the engine is the hardest part of<BR>the job. . This requires two new holes to be drilled and tapped.<BR>Ed Parks provide a detailed description of the swap process on<BR>the Fiero Factory webpage but I would like to provide  the bolt<BR>sizes, tap sizes and drill sizes and explain a home workshop<BR>method of starter relocation. .<BR>There are two  bolts which hold the starter in place-a long one<BR>and a short one. Both of these have M10 x 1.5 threads. The<BR>in-board bolt is the longer of the two. It  is 113-114 mm long<BR>while the shorter outboard one is about 47mm long. Therefore you<BR>will need a M10 x 1.5 hole tap which is tapped into a hole having<BR>a diameter of  8.5mm or .3346" . The closest drill size is size R<BR>(.3390"). You can also get away with a hole made by an 11/32"<BR>drill.. It will produce a slightly looser fit but  it will work.<BR>The best way to locate the starter mounting holes in the home<BR>workshop is to position the starter mounting boss flush with the<BR>edges of the mounting surface of the block and clamp it in place.<BR>With a punch made from an old long M10 bolt ( sharpened tip on<BR>center)  you can "dimple" the block and locate the center of the<BR>inner bolt hole for drilling.. Starting with a new sharp 1/4" HSS<BR>drill bit in a hand held electric drill, drill the inboard hole<BR>through the block. Hold the drill straight with a general purpose<BR>aftermarket drill guide that will keep the drilled hole<BR>perpendicular to the surface. Enlarge the hole with the R size<BR>drill bit and then tap the hole  with the M10 x 1.5 tap in a T<BR>handle wrench. Now bolt the starter in place, check the fit and<BR>tighten the bolt down.<BR>. Now it is time to drill the outboard hole. This is much harder<BR>as you will be drilling on an angled surface. You can solve this<BR>problem by employing the starter as a drill guide. By making a<BR>sleeve that can be inserted into the outboard starter hole it<BR>will guide the drill.. If you do not have a lathe to cut an<BR>insert, an old piece of small pipe or steel fuel line may work<BR>Again drill through the block and tap. Check the fit. If the fit<BR>is wrong this can be remedied by elogating the bolt holes in the<BR>starter to provide some adjustment in position.<BR>Since this procedure may be beyond the scope of some mechanics<BR>there is an alternative solution. Use a local machine shop to<BR>drill the starter holes or bring your engine to The Fiero<BR>Factory.  Ed Parks has all of the equipment necessary and offers<BR>a complete service that will set up your 3.4L block so that it<BR>can be dropped right in. Ed is very proficient at doing complete<BR>3.4L swaps. With this engine and a few simple mods, 200 HP should<BR>be possible. The 3.4L may not be the ultimate swap but it<BR>certeinly appears to be an easy, reliable and cost effective<BR>upgrade for a Fiero.<BR>These engines are found in 93-95 Firebirds and Camaros. I paid<BR>$500 for a junkyard engine with 36K miles on iy. The engine was<BR>super clean and I could have used it as is, but I decided to<BR>rebuild it for a Turbocharger installation. That is another<BR>successful project but too large a story to post here.<BR>Dennis LaGrua<BR>Neshanic, NJ<BR>908-369-6983 home<BR>International Fiero Club/TSFC<BR>Custom FIERO Turbocharging website at: <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> <BR>- Beautiful Fiero Woodgrain Dash kits <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/fierowooddash.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/fierowooddash.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-01-2000||02:36 PM||||Thanks for posting this. This is the kind of feedback I was looking for the other day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Keep on posting,<BR>Roy||||reg||14
